# Telephone Booth (Where Walls Meet Sky) (2026)

A site-specific rotary pay phone installation for the Where Walls Meet Sky exhibition at St. Anne's Anglican Church in Toronto, gathering and recombining voices around loss, rebuilding, and chance communion.

- [Source (Rust rewrite)](https://github.com/djensenius/Telephone-Booth)
- [Operator console source](https://github.com/djensenius/Telephone-Booth-Operator)
- [Operator mobile app source](https://github.com/djensenius/Telephone-Booth-Operator-Mobile)
- [Operator CLI source](https://github.com/djensenius/Telephone-Booth-Operator-cli)
- [Transcription gateway source](https://github.com/djensenius/Telephone-Booth-Transcription)

Presented as part of _Where Walls Meet Sky_, a rotary pay phone and booth waited inside St. Anne's Anglican Church, Toronto: an obsolete public technology placed in a sanctuary transformed by fire. The plate promised free calls. Dial 1 answered a question and left a message; dial 2 played a stranger's answer returned without its original prompt.

**Dates:** August 22 through September 13, 2026  
**Hours:** Thursday through Sunday, 12–8 PM  
**Venue:** St. Anne's Anglican Church  
**Address:** 270 Gladstone Ave, Toronto

For this exhibition, the questions are drawn from St. Anne's itself: from ash, absence, open sky, and the determination to gather and rebuild. The booth turns private speech into a shared acoustic commons, letting voices separate from their authors and recombine into a chorus no single visitor could compose.

The 2026 version is driven by a Rust rewrite of the phone-side software, handling the rotary dial, hook switch, audio recording, playback, and the operator backend that circulates each fragment through the installation.
